$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{x+4}{\sqrt{3x+13}-1}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{x+4}{\sqrt{3x+13}-1}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt{3x+13}+1}{\sqrt{3x+13}+1}\qquad\gray{\text{Rationalize the denominator}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{(x+4)(\sqrt{3x+13}+1)}{(3x+13)-1^2}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{(x+4)}(\sqrt{3x+13}+1)}{3\cancel{(x+4)}}\qquad\gray{\text{Cancel out common factors}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\sqrt{3x+13}+1}{3} \text{, for }x\neq -4
\end{align}$

This means that the two expressions have the same value for all $x$-values (in their domains) except for $-4$.

We can now use the following theorem:

If $f(x)=g(x)$ for all $x$-values in a given interval except for $x=c$, then $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to c}f(x)=\lim_{x\to c}g(x)$.
